App Review Extraction with LangChain
An LLM-powered Python application designed to automatically parse, structure, and analyze app store reviews. It extracts granular insights—including overall sentiment, notable phrases, specific problems, and suggested solutions—and formats them into structured JSON to power product, operations, and marketing dashboards.

🚀 Features
Structured Extraction: Automatically maps unstructured user reviews into a validated Pydantic schema using LangChain.

Granular Sentiment Analysis: Breaks down feedback by specific topics (e.g., UI & UX, Payment & Checkout, Customer Service) rather than just a single blanket score.

Actionable Insights: Separates user complaints into distinct problem statements and suggested solutions.

Rigorous Evaluation: Includes an evaluation script to measure prompt performance across custom validation datasets.

📂 Project Structure
Plaintext
├── app.py          # Main Python application handling model calls and extraction logic
├── prompt.md       # Modular prompt template defining purpose, input, output, and few-shot examples
└── eval.py         # Evaluation script to benchmark and score prompt accuracy against reference data
🛠️ Prompt Architecture (prompt.md)
The prompt follows a robust 7-section design pattern optimized for reliable application prompting:

Purpose: Clearly defines the overarching objective (dashboard data extraction).

Input Description: Specifies the expected format of incoming reviews and warns the model to handle slang, typos, and informal phrasing.

Output Description & Constraints: Enforces strict JSON output formatting, JSON-escaping rules, and strict constraints (e.g., no markdown code block wrappers around the final JSON).

Examples: Provides a comprehensive few-shot example demonstrating how to handle mixed-sentiment reviews, implicit solutions, and categorical mapping.

